{"sections":{"main":{"type":"page","disabled":true,"settings":{}},"673986f0-494b-4464-913d-caf9aa465325":{"type":"banner","settings":{"is_page_hero":false,"background_image_desktop":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/LP-1_Copy-HV_8c022c2d-f255-415b-8f47-8999d9d1afa2.jpg","background_image_tablet":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/LP-1_Copy-HV_8c022c2d-f255-415b-8f47-8999d9d1afa2.jpg","background_image_mobile":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/LP-1-Mobile_Copy-HV_e47cb311-fe3d-43a1-a0bf-eee0fd489d47.jpg","image_overlay_color":"#000000","image_overlay_opacity":0,"link_wrapper":"https:\/\/store.moma.org\/collections\/a-vida-portuguesa","mobile_stack":false,"mobile_stack_color":"#d5edbc","content_position_horizontal_desktop":"left","content_position_vertical_desktop":"start","content_position_vertical_mobile":"start","sub_heading_text":"","screen_reader_text":"Sub-Heading text here","sub_heading_position_desktop":"left","sub_heading_position_mobile":"left","sub_heading_color":"#000000","heading_text":"","heading_position_desktop":"left","heading_position_mobile":"left","heading_color":"#000000","body_text":"","body_text_position_desktop":"left","body_text_position_mobile":"left","body_text_color":"#000000","body_max_width":40,"max_width_unit":"%","cta_text":"","cta_url":"","cta_position_desktop":"left","cta_position_mobile":"left","static_cta_style":"solid_background","cta_static_background_color":"#ffffff","cta_static_text_color":"#000000","hover_cta_style":"transparent_background","cta_hover_background_color":"#ffffff","cta_hover_text_color":"#000000","secondary_cta":false,"cta_text_2":"","cta_url_2":"","static_cta_style_2":"solid_background","cta_static_background_color_2":"#ffffff","cta_static_text_color_2":"#000000","hover_cta_style_2":"transparent_background","cta_hover_background_color_2":"#ffffff","cta_hover_text_color_2":"#000000"}},"5828fb89-0742-4b21-a542-5a62af290be1":{"type":"section-spacer","settings":{"section_spacer_mobile_size":0,"section_spacer_desktop_size":0}},"44da18c7-1e7f-4200-9859-3a38a3e91cbe":{"type":"quote-and-image","settings":{"text":"<p>“We sought out brands, some as old as 100 years, that were disappearing in Portugal and gave them new life.”—Catarina Portas, founder of A Vida Portuguesa<\/p>","image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/Portrait-HV.png","image_position":"right"}},"d0b109ed-89f1-4b7a-98b8-c329e72fcd5a":{"type":"wide-image-and-text","settings":{"headline_text_alignment_desktop":"left","headline_text_alignment_mobile":"left","headline":"Alentejo Cork Stool\/Side Table","text_copy_alignment_desktop":"left","text_copy_alignment_mobile":"left","text":"<p>This rustic design is handcrafted in Alentejo, which is located south of Lisbon. Cork is obtained from the bark of cork oaks, which regenerate themselves, and is extracted without damaging the tree. Portugal is the world’s largest producer of this material, which has a naturally beautiful appearance. <\/p>","cta_text_alignment_desktop":"left","cta_text_alignment_mobile":"left","cta_text":"Shop Now","cta_url":"shopify:\/\/products\/a-vida-portuguesa-alentejo-cork-stool-side-table","anchor":"","custom_cta_styles":false,"cta_background_color":"#000000","cta_text_color":"#ffffff","cta_border_color":"#000000","cta_hover_background_color":"#ffffff","cta_hover_text_color":"#000000","cta_hover_border_color":"#000000","image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/Side_Table_-HV.png","video":"","image_position":"left"}},"d6c5aac8-a8db-48f8-b709-ce800801a9e4":{"type":"wide-image-and-text","custom_css":[],"settings":{"headline_text_alignment_desktop":"left","headline_text_alignment_mobile":"left","headline":"Ceramic Cabbage Bowl & Tureen","text_copy_alignment_desktop":"left","text_copy_alignment_mobile":"left","text":"<p>Conceived in the 1880s by Portuguese designer Rafael Bordalo Pinheiro, these whimsical cabbage designs have been produced in the same factory in Portugal for nearly 140 years. Made from durable ceramic, they’re a delightful way for serving salads and soups.<\/p>","cta_text_alignment_desktop":"left","cta_text_alignment_mobile":"left","cta_text":"Shop Now","cta_url":"shopify:\/\/products\/a-vida-portuguesa-ceramic-cabbage-bowl-large","anchor":"compost","custom_cta_styles":true,"cta_background_color":"#000000","cta_text_color":"#ffffff","cta_border_color":"#000000","cta_hover_background_color":"#ffffff","cta_hover_text_color":"#000000","cta_hover_border_color":"#000000","image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/Ceramic_Cabbage_Bowl_Tureen_-HV.png","video":"","image_position":"right"}},"wide_image_and_text_wYpBzP":{"type":"wide-image-and-text","settings":{"headline_text_alignment_desktop":"left","headline_text_alignment_mobile":"left","headline":"TOM Wooden Figures","text_copy_alignment_desktop":"left","text_copy_alignment_mobile":"left","text":"<p>In 1939, artist Thomaz de Mello, who also went by TOM, conceived a series of hand-painted wooden dolls. Each figure represented a folklore person from different regions of Portugal, a concept which proved to be popular among tourists who were just discovering the country and bringing these home as souvenirs.<\/p>","cta_text_alignment_desktop":"left","cta_text_alignment_mobile":"left","cta_text":"Shop Now","cta_url":"shopify:\/\/products\/a-vida-portuguesa-tom-wooden-figure-22-douro-litoral","anchor":"","custom_cta_styles":false,"cta_background_color":"#000000","cta_text_color":"#ffffff","cta_border_color":"#000000","cta_hover_background_color":"#ffffff","cta_hover_text_color":"#000000","cta_hover_border_color":"#000000","image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/TOM_Wooden_Figures-HV.png","video":"","image_position":"left"}},"wide_image_and_text_RUbM4h":{"type":"wide-image-and-text","settings":{"headline_text_alignment_desktop":"left","headline_text_alignment_mobile":"left","headline":"Regional Cotton Blanket","text_copy_alignment_desktop":"left","text_copy_alignment_mobile":"left","text":"<p>Initially used as protection from the cold by the shepherds in the fields, regional blankets became an essential household in several regions of Portugal. In Minde, a small town in the center of Portugal with a long tradition in the regional development of the clothing industry, the company of Candido Alves produced this blanket from 100% cotton. <\/p>","cta_text_alignment_desktop":"left","cta_text_alignment_mobile":"left","cta_text":"Shop Now","cta_url":"shopify:\/\/products\/a-vida-portuguesa-regional-cotton-blanket","anchor":"","custom_cta_styles":true,"cta_background_color":"#ffffff","cta_text_color":"#000000","cta_border_color":"#000000","cta_hover_background_color":"#000000","cta_hover_text_color":"#ffffff","cta_hover_border_color":"#000000","image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/Regional_Cotton_Blanket_-HV.png","video":"","image_position":"right"}},"wide_image_and_text_793xLc":{"type":"wide-image-and-text","settings":{"headline_text_alignment_desktop":"left","headline_text_alignment_mobile":"left","headline":"Colorful Reed Basket Bag","text_copy_alignment_desktop":"left","text_copy_alignment_mobile":"left","text":"<p>In Portugal, the craft of reed weaving was a dying art until it was rescued by heritage brand Toino Abel. Working side by side with Portugal’s last remaining weavers, the brand’s workers learned this traditional craft. This vibrant bag features leather top handles and shoulder strap.<\/p>","cta_text_alignment_desktop":"left","cta_text_alignment_mobile":"left","cta_text":"Shop Now","cta_url":"shopify:\/\/products\/a-vida-portuguesa-colorful-reed-basket-bag","anchor":"","custom_cta_styles":false,"cta_background_color":"#000000","cta_text_color":"#ffffff","cta_border_color":"#000000","cta_hover_background_color":"#ffffff","cta_hover_text_color":"#000000","cta_hover_border_color":"#000000","image":"shopify:\/\/shop_images\/Colorful_Reed_Basket_Bag_-HV.png","video":"","image_position":"left"}},"wide_image_and_text_kpMtUP":{"type":"wide-image-and-text","settings":{"headline_text_alignment_desktop":"left","headline_text_alignment_mobile":"left","headline":"Meet Catarina Portas","tex